[{"_1":2},"llms/mdx",{"_3":4},"data","# EST reference (/docs/platform/iot-fleets/enrollment/est-reference)\n\n\n\nReference for the [examined backend](/docs/platform/iot-fleets/enrollment/overview#contract-version), based on [RFC 7030](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc7030.html). Use [EST client](/docs/platform/iot-fleets/enrollment/est-client) for the procedure and [DMS](/docs/platform/iot-fleets/enrollment/dms) to configure policy.\n\nEndpoint reference [#endpoint-reference]\n\nPublic route in the examples: `/.well-known/est/{APS}/{operation}`. `APS` is the DMS ID. The router admits routes without that segment, but the controller requires it; do not use them as a default service. Gateway configuration determines the public host and route.\n\n| Operation         | Method | Authentication and condition                                                                   |\n| ----------------- | ------ | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `/cacerts`        | GET    | DMS reading does not require an EST credential; HTTPS and infrastructure controls still apply. |\n| `/simpleenroll`   | POST   | Initial authentication and DMS registration/replacement policy.                                |\n| `/simplereenroll` | POST   | Renewal authentication, existing identity, subject, revocation and window.                     |\n| `/serverkeygen`   | POST   | Initial authentication and admission; server generation enabled.                               |\n\nThese routes do not implement `/csrattrs` or `/fullcmc`. There is no native JWT EST authentication mode; a webhook can interpret a request credential according to its own contract.\n\ncacerts [#cacerts]\n\nThe default response has `Content-Type: application/pkcs7-mime; smime-type=certs-only` and `Content-Transfer-Encoding: base64`. The body is Base64-encoded DER PKCS#7 with lines of up to 76 characters. It includes the certificates selected by the DMS, not necessarily every ancestor.\n\n`Accept: application/x-pem-file` requests the bundle in PEM through a Lamassu extension. Inspect and approve its contents before modifying trusted roots.\n\nsimpleenroll [#simpleenroll]\n\nSend a Base64-encoded DER PKCS#10 CSR with `Content-Type: application/pkcs10`. The examined controller compares that media type literally; avoid adding parameters such as charset. The CN identifies the device and the CSR signature must be verified through DMS policy.\n\nThe default response uses the PKCS#7/Base64 format described for cacerts, but contains the issued certificate; do not assume it includes its entire chain. `Accept: application/x-pem-file` returns that certificate in PEM. The client retains the key that signed the CSR.\n\nsimplereenroll [#simplereenroll]\n\nUse the same request and certificate response formats. Authentication comes from the independent `reenrollment_settings.est_rfc7030_settings` block. With a client certificate, its CN must match the CSR CN; the subject is also checked against the current certificate.\n\nThe enrollment CA or an additional CA validates the credential in mTLS mode. The expiry check through `enable_expired_renewal` belongs to that validator. A revoked current certificate and a request before the window are rejected. See [Renewal](/docs/platform/iot-fleets/enrollment/renewal-and-recovery) for installation and recovery.\n\nserverkeygen [#serverkeygen]\n\nThe server generates a software key, rebuilds the CSR with that key and performs initial enrollment. The flag must be enabled and the algorithm permitted by the profile. This operation's key does not become a hardware key because the CA is hosted in an HSM.\n\nThe code supports RSA and ECDSA with 224, 256, 384 or 521 bits. An unrecognized ECDSA size can fall back to 256; explicitly select a compatible size and check the resulting key. Do not reuse an enrolled ID to test this operation without reviewing replacement.\n\nResponse format [#response-format]\n\nThe body is **MIME multipart/mixed**, with the boundary specified in `Content-Type`. It contains two parts:\n\n| Part                                            | Content after decoding its Base64             |\n| ----------------------------------------------- | --------------------------------------------- |\n| `application/pkcs8`                             | Unencrypted DER PKCS#8 private key.           |\n| `application/pkcs7-mime; smime-type=certs-only` | DER PKCS#7 containing the issued certificate. |\n\nThe controller also adds `Content-Transfer-Encoding: base64` to the outer header, although the outer body is MIME text. **Do not Base64-decode the entire body.** Read the actual boundary and decode each part according to its headers.\n\nRequest and extraction [#request-and-extraction]\n\nIn a Bash session with `set -euo pipefail` and `umask 077`, use `EST_BASE` and the trust/bootstrap files from [EST client](/docs/platform/iot-fleets/enrollment/est-client). Select an available and admitted `est-serverkeygen-001`. The `server-request.key` only signs the input CSR; the received identity uses the server-generated key.\n\n```bash\ntest ! -e server-request.key\nopenssl genpkey -algorithm EC -pkeyopt ec_paramgen_curve:P-256 -out server-request.key\nopenssl req -new -sha256 -key server-request.key \\\n  -subj '/CN=est-serverkeygen-001' -out serverkeygen.csr\nopenssl req -in serverkeygen.csr -outform DER -out serverkeygen.csr.der\nopenssl base64 -A -in serverkeygen.csr.der -out serverkeygen.csr.b64\ncurl --fail-with-body --show-error --silent --max-time 30 \\\n  --cacert est-trust.pem --cert bootstrap.crt --key bootstrap.key \\\n  --header 'Content-Type: application/pkcs10' \\\n  --data-binary @serverkeygen.csr.b64 \\\n  --dump-header serverkeygen.headers --output serverkeygen.mime \\\n  \"$EST_BASE/serverkeygen\"\n```\n\nSave this Python 3 extractor. It validates both parts before writing and rejects existing output files:\n\n```python\n# Save as extract-serverkeygen.py and run: python3 extract-serverkeygen.py\nimport base64\nimport os\nfrom email import policy\nfrom email.parser import BytesParser\nfrom pathlib import Path\n\nos.umask(0o077)\nheaders = Path(\"serverkeygen.headers\").read_bytes().splitlines()\ncontent_types = [\n    line.split(b\":\", 1)[1].strip()\n    for line in headers\n    if line.lower().startswith(b\"content-type:\")\n]\nif not content_types:\n    raise SystemExit(\"Missing response Content-Type\")\nmessage = BytesParser(policy=policy.default).parsebytes(\n    b\"Content-Type: \" + content_types[-1]\n    + b\"\\r\\nMIME-Version: 1.0\\r\\n\\r\\n\"\n    + Path(\"serverkeygen.mime\").read_bytes()\n)\nif message.get_content_type() != \"multipart/mixed\" or not message.is_multipart():\n    raise SystemExit(\"Expected multipart/mixed\")\nif message.defects:\n    raise SystemExit(\"Malformed multipart response\")\nexpected = {\n    \"application/pkcs8\": \"device.server.key.der\",\n    \"application/pkcs7-mime\": \"device.server.p7b\",\n}\noutputs = {}\nfor part in message.iter_parts():\n    media_type = part.get_content_type()\n    if media_type not in expected or media_type in outputs or part.defects:\n        raise SystemExit(\"Unexpected, duplicate or malformed part\")\n    encoding = str(part.get(\"Content-Transfer-Encoding\", \"\")).strip().lower()\n    payload = part.get_payload()\n    if encoding != \"base64\" or not isinstance(payload, str):\n        raise SystemExit(\"Expected Base64 part\")\n    try:\n        decoded = base64.b64decode(\"\".join(payload.split()), validate=True)\n    except (ValueError, UnicodeError) as error:\n        raise SystemExit(\"Invalid Base64 part\") from error\n    if not decoded:\n        raise SystemExit(\"Empty part\")\n    outputs[media_type] = decoded\nif set(outputs) != set(expected):\n    raise SystemExit(\"Missing key or certificate part\")\nif any(Path(name).exists() for name in expected.values()):\n    raise SystemExit(\"Output already exists; use a fresh directory\")\nfor media_type, name in expected.items():\n    with open(name, \"xb\") as output:\n        output.write(outputs[media_type])\n```\n\nConvert and check the result:\n\n```bash\npython3 extract-serverkeygen.py\nopenssl pkcs8 -inform DER -in device.server.key.der -nocrypt -out device.server.key\nopenssl pkcs7 -inform DER -in device.server.p7b -print_certs -quiet -out device.server.crt\nopenssl verify -CAfile device-root.pem -purpose sslclient device.server.crt\nopenssl x509 -in device.server.crt -pubkey -noout > server-certificate-public.pem\nopenssl pkey -pubin -in server-certificate-public.pem -outform DER -out server-certificate-public.der\nopenssl pkey -in device.server.key -pubout -outform DER -out server-key-public.der\ncmp server-certificate-public.der server-key-public.der\n```\n\nInspect the CN, serial, dates and uses; check association in Lamassu before installing. Protect the DER and PEM files containing the key and remove temporary copies according to the device's storage mechanism. See [openssl-pkcs8](https://docs.openssl.org/3.6/man1/openssl-pkcs8/) and the [Python MIME parser](https://docs.python.org/3/library/email.parser.html).\n\nCompatibility notes [#compatibility-notes]\n\nDo not interpret every 500 as a transient failure. Invalid input typically produces 400; service denials and errors typically produce 500 with a JSON `err` body. Gateway errors and some controller paths can have another format. Retain headers and body before decoding and see [troubleshooting](/docs/platform/iot-fleets/enrollment/troubleshooting).\n\nThe examples use [openssl-pkcs7](https://docs.openssl.org/3.6/man1/openssl-pkcs7/) to extract certificates and [openssl-x509](https://docs.openssl.org/3.6/man1/openssl-x509/) to prepare renewal. Check your version's capabilities and responses before integrating a generic EST client.\n"]
